"help.topic.virtualMachines" = "Virtual Machines";
"help.vm.intro" = "A virtual machine's profile is a whole Mac: its own macOS with its own Safari, disk, keychain, serial number and network card, in a window. Where an ordinary profile is a separate set of website data, a machine shares nothing of its system with any other profile, and sites see a real Safari on a real macOS.";
"help.vm.add" = "Adding the machines";
"help.vm.step.open" = "Signed in to your account, open Virtual Machines in the Profiles window's sidebar and press Add Virtual Machines.";
"help.vm.step.download" = "Lumen asks Apple which macOS this Mac can run and downloads it from Apple's servers — about 25 GB, over six connections at once. It can be paused; it goes on from where it stopped, also after a quit.";
"help.vm.step.setup" = "macOS is installed (10–20 minutes) and the new Mac opens in a window. Go through its own setup: the country, an account with a password; choose Set Up Later for Apple Account and leave FileVault off.";
"help.vm.step.tools" = "In the machine's Finder open My Shared Files (Go → Computer) and open Install Lumen Tools; type the account's password. The machine shuts down by itself: the base system is ready.";
"help.vm.step.create" = "Press New Machine, give it a name and a proxy. Each machine is made from the base system in seconds at its first start. For another machine like one you have, ⋯ → New Machine with These Settings… opens the same settings under a new name: a new Mac, with nothing of the first one's data.";
"help.vm.what" = "What a machine has of its own";
"help.vm.what.system" = "Its own macOS and Safari: cookies, history, the keychain, caches, the DNS cache — everything a Mac keeps, on a disk of its own.";
"help.vm.what.proxy" = "With a proxy, everything the machine sends goes through it — the whole macOS, not only Safari — and nothing any other way. The proxy is checked before each start; if it does not answer, the machine is not started.";
"help.vm.what.noProxy" = "Without a proxy the machine goes out as this Mac's own apps do: through its VPN, and through the proxy in its network settings when a VPN tool has put itself there. If a machine has no internet, its row in the list says why.";
"help.vm.what.locale" = "The time zone and the language are the machine's own system settings. With a proxy they are taken from where the proxy comes out, at each start. Without one you choose: this Mac's, those of the country this Mac's own connection comes out in (its VPN, when one is on), or your own.";
"help.vm.what.screen" = "The screen is one of real Macs' — MacBook Air, MacBook Pro, iMac — whatever the size of the window on your Mac.";
"help.vm.what.look" = "The system's look and habits are the machine's own too: light or dark appearance, the accent colour, the computer's name, the scroll bars, the Dock, the clock and the week's first day, the keyboard layout (by the machine's language, unless you choose another) — and the desktop picture, which is Lumen's wallpaper drawn for the machine's screen unless you switch it off in the machine's settings. While a machine runs, Lumen compares the time zone and language its macOS really has with what was set, and says so in the list if they differ.";
"help.vm.what.network" = "In a machine's settings you can also block sites for its whole macOS, give its network card another address, and open several pages at start.";
"help.vm.what.wake" = "Instant wake (in a machine's settings): closing the machine keeps it exactly as it is, and opening it brings it back in a few seconds — the same pages, the same place. It takes about as much disk as the machine has memory while it sleeps, and such a machine has no sound. “Start Anew” in the machine's menu gives the kept state up.";
"help.vm.what.browser" = "Browser mode: the machine is shown as a browser window — Safari over its whole screen, no menu bar, no Dock, its desktop covered while it starts. If Safari is not full screen, “Safari Full Screen” in the machine's menu puts it so.";
"help.vm.what.livenetwork" = "“Network…” in a running machine's menu shows every host its macOS connects to, how much went each way, and what was blocked — and blocks a host with one click. Names and amounts only; nothing of it is kept.";
"help.vm.costs" = "What it takes";
"help.vm.costs.disk" = "Disk: about 25 GB for the base system; each machine then takes only what changes in it, usually 3–6 GB. The downloaded image is removed after the installation.";
"help.vm.costs.memory" = "Memory: 3–8 GB per running machine (in its settings), taken only while it runs. A new machine gets what this Mac can spare — 8 GB on a Mac with 24 GB or more, 6 with 16, otherwise 4. With 4 GB, macOS in the machine stutters once a few pages are open.";
"help.vm.costs.limit" = "Two machines at once: Apple's limit for macOS in virtual machines on one Mac.";
"help.vm.costs.start" = "A machine starts in 20–40 seconds, as a Mac does; Shut Down closes its macOS properly.";
"help.vm.costs.hidden" = "A machine's macOS draws its screen even while its window is hidden. Screen off while hidden (in a machine's settings, under Device) puts its display to sleep once the window has been out of sight for half a minute, which spares this Mac; it wakes a second or two after the window is seen again. Meanwhile pages in the machine are hidden, as on a Mac whose display slept.";
"help.vm.know" = "Good to know";
"help.vm.know.disks" = "The machines' disks are not encrypted by Lumen's vault: they are files in the vault's folder. Turn on FileVault on your Mac to have them encrypted. Names, proxies and their passwords are in the vault.";
"help.vm.know.udp" = "UDP — HTTP/3 (QUIC) and WebRTC — goes through the machine's proxy when the proxy carries it: a SOCKS5 proxy with UDP, checked before every start; WebRTC then shows the proxy's address. With an HTTP proxy, or one that does not carry UDP, they are off: UDP never goes around the proxy. Without a proxy it goes out as this Mac's own, as the machine's pages do. DNS and the time are always answered inside Lumen.";
"help.vm.know.updates" = "Automatic macOS updates are off in the machines: each would download gigabytes. To move to a newer macOS, remove the base system (⋯) and add it again; the machines you have keep theirs.";
"help.vm.know.speed" = "The download's speed is that of the way between your connection and Apple's servers; a VPN or proxy on this Mac slows it, since the download goes through them. If it is slow: turn the VPN off while it downloads, or try another network. Or use a file you have: \"I have the file…\" takes macOS's restore image (.ipsw) downloaded on another Mac, by a download manager (\"Copy Apple's link\" while it downloads gives the address), or given by a colleague — Apple checks it when it installs, and your file is not moved or changed.";
"help.vm.know.base" = "⋯ → Open the Base System starts the base itself, to change what every new machine should have. Machines made before keep what they had.";
"help.vm.know.license" = "Apple's license allows macOS in virtual machines on Apple hardware for development, testing and personal non-commercial use.";
"help.vm.know.local" = "The machines stay on this Mac: they are not synced and not part of a profile export.";
